== Findings that drive the design

*Ball loss is a state, not noise.* Once the ball disappears it tends to stay missing: the median gap is 10.7 s and 85% of missing frames fall in gaps longer than 10 s (Table I). Simply holding the last label across a gap of that length is unsafe, because stoppages and turnovers happen inside it. The model therefore needs temporal context of tens of seconds and must use player configuration when the ball is absent.

*Nearest-player reasoning is ambiguous exactly where it matters.* In 32.5% of visible-ball frames the nearest home and away players are within 2 m of each other's distance to the ball. Our decoder accordingly adds proximity evidence only when the ball is visible and a player is very close to it (gate $r$ in @sec:decoder). Everywhere else it relies on the learned temporal posterior.

*Tracks are not players.* Tracklets have a median length of 13.6 s but a P90 of about 200 s, so per-slot features are unstable. All features are therefore team-level aggregates or permutation-invariant sets.

*Auxiliary signals are weak or unsynchronised.* The Smart Tagging `Team` tag names the *defending* side for "defending" codes. Read naively, it agrees with a nearest-player baseline on only 53.7% of frames; after flipping those codes, agreement rises to 63.4%. Broadcast homography is valid on only 65.8% of frames, and the broadcast video is not synchronised to the tactical timeline. Neither signal is an input to the selected method.

== Public ground truth

The public GT covers frames 11,185–18,684 (7,500 frames): 3,454 H, 2,577 A and 1,469 D, forming 17 possessions with a mean duration of #fx(N.public_report.gt_mean_spell_duration_s, d: 1) s. Six of the 17 last less than 4 s (@fig:durations a). One missed possession moves spellF1 by roughly six points, so this window was used only to check frame alignment and was never used for tuning.

