  placement: top,
  scope: "parent",
) <fig:pipeline>

== Observations

The prepared inputs, derived from automatic tracking, contain team-centric frame channels, ball channels and an unordered set of per-player feature vectors. Each is computed from both teams' perspectives (two mirrored views), so the same network predicts "my team has the ball". Video, Smart Tagging, homography and any human event labels are *not* inference inputs (Table II).

== Short-spell-weighted PP-TCN (weight #tcn.weight)

The backbone is a residual temporal convolutional network @bai2018tcn. A $1 times 1$ input projection to #tcn.config.ch channels is followed by six residual blocks (GroupNorm, dilated convolution with kernel 5 and dilations $(1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 1)$, GELU, dropout #tcn.config.dropout). A per-player branch encodes each player's 10-d vector with a two-layer MLP. It pools the encodings over the set with both attention and a masked max, which makes the branch permutation-invariant @zaheer2017deepsets, and adds the result to the temporal stream. Two logits are produced per frame: $z_D$ (dead ball) and $z_T$ (the viewing team has possession). They are trained with binary cross-entropy: $z_D$ on all labelled frames, $z_T$ on live frames only.

The wave5 change is the *short-possession loss*. The team-state loss is multiplied by 2 on every live spell whose *complete* duration within the half is under 4 s. Durations are measured on the full record, not on the training crop, so fragments cut by a crop or by unknown labels are not falsely up-weighted. Training uses AdamW @loshchilov2017adamw (lr #tcn.config.lr, weight decay #tcn.config.weight_decay), a OneCycle schedule @smith2017onecycle, 1,500 updates of batch 16 on 384-sample crops, full-ball masking with probability #tcn.config.mask_full, and seeds 0, 1 and 2, whose outputs are averaged.

== Set-sequence BiGRU (weight #gru.weight)

The second component is the hybrid model of an earlier campaign (wave3): a player-set encoder followed by a two-layer bidirectional GRU @cho2014gru @schuster1997brnn (hidden size 32, window 300, 8 epochs, seed 2027) with an auxiliary boundary head. It is trained on the 5 IDSSE development matches (3 corruption rounds each) on CPU with one thread. It is trained from scratch with this recipe for the submitted file. The joint probabilities $p(H), p(A), p(D)$ of the two components are mixed 75/25 and renormalised.

== Rate-calibrated segment decoder (`dead45`) <sec:decoder>

*Dead mask.* A frame is dead if $p(D) > #dec.dead_threshold$ and it belongs to a run lasting at least #dec.min_dead_s s. No prior shift is applied to the dead probability.

*Team evidence.* For live frames we form log-scores $ell_H = kappa log p_T$ and $ell_A = kappa log(1 - p_T)$ with $kappa = #dec.kappa$. When the ball is visible and the nearest player is within $r = #dec.gate_r$ m, we add $-log(1 + e^(-m))$ to $ell_H$ and $-log(1 + e^(m))$ to $ell_A$, with margin $m = (d_A - d_H) \/ tau$ and $tau = #dec.tau$.

*Viterbi.* A two-state Viterbi pass @viterbi1967 with switch cost $lambda$ decodes H/A. It is *reset in every live interval*, so a stoppage never carries a possession across it. Rather than fixing $lambda$, we pick it from an 18-value grid so that the number of decoded possessions matches a target $hat(n) = rho dot T_"graded"$. Here $rho$ is the possession rate per minute estimated from the *training folds only*, balanced across providers, and possessions are counted after merging. Finally, live runs shorter than #dec.merge_s s are absorbed into the preceding run. No rule specific to the target match (forced dead balls, kickoff handling) is used. The match-half boundaries come from the competition metadata.

