== From-scratch reproduction <sec:retrain>

The submitted CSV was produced by one notebook (`experiments/E06_wave5/E06_wave5.ipynb`, run on an NVIDIA L40S). It downloads IDSSE and SkillCorner, rebuilds the training packs and features, trains the BiGRU (260 s, CPU) and the PP-TCN (79 s, GPU), rebuilds the target inputs from the raw match bundle and decodes, calling the campaigns' own functions. The rebuilt training packs give the same provider-balanced statistics as the campaign (possession rate #RT.run.training_stats_provider_balanced.rate_per_min per minute, dead share #RT.run.training_stats_provider_balanced.dead_share). Two exactness checks pass inside the run: a per-seed re-implementation matches the model's prediction (max. abs. difference $1.8 times 10^(-7)$), and a step-by-step rebuild of the decoder equals its output.

The submitted file has #RT.full_match_possessions full-match possessions (median #fx(N.durations.w5_full_median, d: 1) s) and scores public spellF1 #fx(spub.spellF1) and macroF1 #fx(spub.macroF1). Retraining on other hardware or library versions can change the weights slightly, so a rerun is expected to agree closely with the submitted file but not byte for byte. The development and holdout results above come from the selection campaign's own training run.

= Explainability <sec:xai>

All numbers and figures in this section come from the executed notebook run and describe the submitted model. Public-window values are diagnostics on 17 possessions; one possession moves spellF1 by about 0.06.

*Anatomy of a decision.* @fig:anatomy follows the public window through every stage. The two components agree on dead/live for 92.0% of samples and on the team for 85.3%; fusion mostly smooths the TCN with the BiGRU. The decoder's team evidence is dominated by the network: the proximity gate fires on only 4.3% of samples over the full match (7.0% in the public window).

#figure(
  image("../../image/fig10_decision_anatomy.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [One decision through every stage on the public window: (A) dead-ball probability of each component and the dead mask; (B) P(home | live) of each component; (C) decoder team evidence $ell_H - ell_A$ and the samples where the proximity gate fired; (D) labels after Viterbi, after the 1-s merge, and the GT.],
  placement: top,
  scope: "parent",
) <fig:anatomy>

*Stage ablation.* Changing one stage at a time (Table IV) shows what each part does. Removing the 1-s merge fragments the output (352 possessions; public spellF1 0.761). Giving the BiGRU more weight over-segments sharply (weight 0.5: 330 possessions, 0.700; BiGRU alone: 388, 0.566). The TCN alone scores higher on the public window (0.786), within the noise of one possession; we did not change the pre-registered method after seeing it. The calibrated switch cost sits at the smallest grid value (λ = 0.05) and still yields 302 possessions against the 386 implied by the training rate, so the possession count is limited by the dead mask and the merge rather than by λ.

