= Discussion and Limitations

*What works.* The decoder does as much as the network. Calibrating the possession count from training statistics, resetting Viterbi in every live interval and merging sub-second runs turn a good frame classifier into a good *segmenter*. This is why spellF1 rises from 0.33 (smooth50) to 0.77 on the public window while macroF1 rises much less (0.62 → 0.90). Weighting short spells in the loss is a cheap way to counter the decoder's bias toward fewer, longer possessions.

*What does not.* (i) Short possessions are still mostly missed. Recall at IoU 0.75 stays below 25% in every cohort, and on the public window it was short exchanges that were lost. (ii) When the ball disappears more often, the method creates more spurious possessions and loses some long ones. This matters because ball loss on the target match is severe (Table I). (iii) Dead-ball onsets are detected late. (iv) The predicted full-match dead share (#pct(N.durations.full_share.D)) cannot be validated without the private GT.

*Validity.* All external holdout matches and the public window had been seen in earlier campaigns. The stress cohort is a transformation of matches already in the holdout, not new matches. The results are therefore adaptive estimates, not unbiased ones. We found no path by which competition GT flows into training, inference or selection, but this audit does not certify that the method is leakage-free. Metrica and SkillCorner labels are proxies (events and phases of play), not the corrected-tracking definition used by the target GT. The full-match (private) score is unknown.

*Reproducibility.* The submitted CSV is identified by its SHA-256 (Table II). It is produced end-to-end from public training data and the raw match bundle by one notebook (@sec:retrain). Retraining on other hardware or library versions can change the weights slightly, so a rerun is expected to agree closely with the submitted file but not byte for byte.

= Conclusion

The selected method treats possession as segmentation. A short-spell-weighted PP-TCN, a small set-sequence BiGRU and a rate-calibrated, interval-reset Viterbi decoder were chosen by a pre-registered, provider-balanced rule over 96 configurations. The result is a #sgn(dev.delta_vs_wave4) gain in development spellF1 and a public diagnostic spellF1 of #fx(spub.spellF1) for the submitted file, against #fx(row("baseline_smooth50").spell_f1_mean) for the official baseline. The open problem is the trade-off between short and long possessions when the ball is lost. Progress on it needs fresh external validation rather than further tuning on the 17 public possessions.
